---
description: Keep repository changes narrowly scoped, causally related, reviewable, and independently understandable.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Atomic Change

Keep each change to the smallest complete diff that satisfies one coherent
outcome.

Before editing, identify the purpose, affected package, required supporting
changes, tests, generated outputs, and known out-of-scope work. Every changed
file and section must directly support that outcome. Exclude work that is
merely nearby, cleaner, or convenient.

> Would the requested behavior be incomplete, incorrect, or untestable without
> this change?

If no, keep it out of scope. Expand scope only when implementation proves the
additional change is necessary.

Before introducing a helper, abstraction, module, type, or dependency:

1. Search for an existing solution.
2. Check package boundaries and dependencies.
3. Reuse existing infrastructure when it correctly solves the task.
4. Add new structure only when required.

Preserve observable behavior unless intentionally changing it, including errors,
serialization, ordering, persistence, concurrency, and side effects. Preserve
public APIs by default; inspect exports, consumers, serialization, and
compatibility before changing shared contracts.

For generated files, change the source of truth and use the official generator.
For dependencies, update only when required and include the lockfile
consequence.

Tests protecting changed behavior belong in scope. Never delete tests, weaken
assertions, narrow valid property domains, add arbitrary sleeps or retries, or
mock away the system under test.

Before completion, inspect `git status`, `git diff --stat`, `git diff`, and
`git diff --check`. At 20 or more changed files, reassess whether all files are
required for one coherent change.

Do not revert, reset, clean, stage, amend, or overwrite pre-existing worktree
changes. Stop when the requested behavior, required validation, and final diff
review are complete.
